// NOISEQ.QS0 - how noise distorts a circuit, and readout mitigation.
//
// Demonstrates: the sampled-Kraus noise models (--noise-*) and readout
// mitigation (--mitigate-readout).
//
// Qubit-level explanation:
//   The circuit prepares the Bell state (|00>+|11>)/sqrt2, so an ideal run
//   reports exactly 50/50 |00> and |11>. The explicit MEASURE ops are what
//   the noise models act on: readout error perturbs the reported outcome of
//   a MEASURE, while depolarizing error applies a random Pauli after every
//   single-qubit gate.
//
//   --noise-depolarizing 0.05 breaks the perfect correlation: after H 0 a
//   random Pauli acts on qubit 0 with probability 0.05, so the histogram
//   gains |01> and |10> counts.
//
//   --noise-readout 0.1 makes each MEASURE mis-report its outcome with
//   probability 0.1, tilting the |00>/|11> ratio.
//
//   --mitigate-readout inverts the per-qubit confusion matrix to estimate
//   the ideal counts from the noisy histogram. It is an estimator, so the
//   recovered counts can differ slightly from the shot total.
//
//   Noise runs on the CPU reference path only (deterministic per seed).
//
// Run:
//   plasma run examples/NOISEQ.QS0 --shots 1000                     (ideal)
//   plasma run examples/NOISEQ.QS0 --noise-depolarizing 0.05 --shots 1000
//   plasma run examples/NOISEQ.QS0 --noise-readout 0.1 --shots 1000
//   plasma run examples/NOISEQ.QS0 --noise-readout 0.1 --mitigate-readout --shots 1000
QUBITS 2
H 0
CNOT 0 1
MEASURE 0 -> c0
MEASURE 1 -> c1
SHOT 1000
PRINT
END